<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jizya</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jizya was a tax levied in Islamic states on non-Muslim subjects, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhimmi">dhimmi</a>, in return for protection and exemption from military service. It was established in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate</a> and continued in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a>, where it was an important source of revenue. It was also collected in later states such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Jizya, from an Arabic word probably derived from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-persian">Middle Persian</a> gazītak, a tax on the lower classes of Sasanian Persia, was levied on non-Muslim subjects of a state governed by Islamic law. The Muslim Arab conquerors largely kept the taxation systems of the Sasanian and Byzantine empires, and its origins are complex because the system arising from the conquests was not uniform but resulted from a variety of agreements. William Montgomery Watt traced it to a pre-Islamic practice in which a powerful Arabian tribe protected weaker neighbours for tribute, refunded if protection failed.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and Christians in parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> began paying tribute called jizya during Muhammad's lifetime, at first as a share of produce and fixed goods rather than a poll tax. In the first century of expansion jizya and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharaj">kharaj</a> were each used for individual taxes, land taxes and collective tribute.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadith">hadiths</a> mention jizya without a limiting rate. Classical jurists understood it as payment for the protection the ruler gave, for exemption from military service and for permission to practise another faith with some communal autonomy. Most required it only of adult, free, sane, able-bodied men, and exempted women, children, the old, the sick, monks, slaves and non-Muslim foreigners staying temporarily; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhimmi">dhimmis</a> who served in the army did not pay, and in some accounts the poor paid nothing. The rate was not fixed. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harun-al-rashid">Harun al-Rashid</a> the jurist Abu Yusuf set amounts of 48 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dirham">dirhams</a> for the richest, 24 for those of moderate wealth and 12 for craftsmen and labourers, and payment could be made in kind.</p><p>The Bayt al-Mal, the public treasury, collected it, and in later times its revenues were commonly allocated to scholars. Jurists differed on its purpose: some saw a payment for protection, others an incentive to convert, and by the end of the 8th century some regarded it as a mark of inferior status.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the Indian subcontinent Islamic rulers imposed jizya from the 11th century, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a> expanded it. After the Norman conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> a poll tax on the Muslim minority, locally called gisia, continued the jizya of the earlier Muslim rulers of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> jizya from Christian and Jewish communities was among the main sources of tax income; in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> it was paid by the community collectively as maqtu. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> the Zoroastrian minority paid it until 1884, when pressure from the Persian Zoroastrian Amelioration Fund led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a> government to remove it. It was eliminated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a> in the 19th century, with French colonisation, and continued in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> until the first decade of the 20th century. No Muslim state imposes it now.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jizya” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Christians and Jews were tolerated as dhimmis but paid the <b>jizya</b> and the kharaj; about half the population was Muslim by the Norman Conquest.</blockquote><a href="/empire/emirate-of-sicily">Emirate of Sicily · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Akbar’s policy of Sulh-i-kul (“peace with all”), together with his abolition of the <b>jizya</b> on non-Muslims in 1564, reduced sectarian tensions and broadened the base of imperial support.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>jizya</b>, a poll tax on non-Muslim subjects, generated further revenue and created a fiscal disincentive to mass conversion, since widespread conversion would have reduced income, though this was never formal policy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…a layered fiscal structure: each of the caliphate's thirty or so emirates collected taxes according to Islamic principles, including zakat on produce and livestock, <b>jizya</b> on non-Muslim subjects and kharaj on agricultural land, remitting a portion to Sokoto while retaining the remainder for local administration and military expenditure.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Raj Singh I (r. 1652–1680) opposed Aurangzeb’s reimposition of the <b>jizya</b> tax and supported the Rathores of Marwar in the Rajput war of 1679–1681, which ended in a compromise peace.</blockquote><a href="/empire/udaipur-state">Udaipur State · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>jizya</b> poll tax on non-Muslim subjects (dhimmis) and the kharaj land tax were the main sources of revenue.</blockquote><a href="/empire/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
